Say what the root nub is called

The root nub takes its name from the device tree: InitIOKit() hands the
tree to IOPlatformExpertDevice::init(), which makes a registry entry out of
it and uses that entry as the nub's name.  So the name says whether the
tree was found at all.

If it is not "device-tree", then nothing that matches on that name is ever
offered the nub -- the platform expert that reads the tree never starts,
the nodes under /arm-io are never published, and the storage controller is
never matched.  That is the whole chain, and the name is where it can be
seen from.

The line is printed next to the one that publishes the personalities,
because that is where the matching starts and because the output there
reaches the screen.

---

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
@@ -245,6 +245,12 @@
 
 			printf("\nnio: publishing %d static personalities",
 			       (int) personalities->getCount());
+			/* nio: the root nub takes its name from the device tree, so its
+			 * name says whether the tree was found at all.  If it is not
+			 * "device-tree", nothing that matches on that name is ever
+			 * offered the nub, and the drivers below stay unmatched. */
+			printf("\nnio: root nub is named %s",
+			       gRootNub->getName() ? gRootNub->getName() : "(none)");
 			gIOCatalogue->addDrivers(personalities, false);
 		}
 	}
